```python
report = {
    "math": {"marks": 95, "grade": "A1", "remark": "Excellent"},
    "science": {"marks": 88, "grade": "A2", "remark": "Good"},
    "english": {"marks": 92, "grade": "A1", "remark": "Very Good"}
}
```
Keys = subject names (strings) — immutable, meaningful, unique. Values = nested dictionaries for structured data. Average = `sum(v["marks"] for v in report.values()) / len(report)`. If a student uses a list of dictionaries `[{"subject": "math", ...}]`, they lose O(1) subject-wise access and complicate average calculation — showing they haven't internalized *key-based design*.
